  "id": "COMPOUND:17374 - PROTEIN:5954",
  "verbalization": "Mollicellin N appears to have a direct relationship with Ubiquitin carboxyl-terminal hydrolase isozyme L3 through a series of intermediate targets. The evidence suggests that Mollicellin N acts as a target for 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid, which in turn targets Ubiquitin carboxyl-terminal hydrolase isozyme L3. This pathway is consistent across multiple paths, with varying levels of confidence, but all pointing to a shared mechanism of action involving the same intermediate compound. The presence of multiple targets for 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid indicates a potential role in modulating the activity of the ubiquitin protease, which is a key process in cellular homeostasis. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that Mollicellin N influences the ubiquitin protease system, potentially through the activation or inhibition of 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid. The evidence does not exclude alternative pathways, but the consistent involvement of 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid as a mediator strengthens the proposed mechanism.",
